$('#app').addEventListener('change',async e=>{if(e.target.id!=='book-file')return;const file=e.target.files[0];if(!file)return;if(!/\.txt$/i.test(file.name)){toast('Choose a plain .txt file. PDF and EPUB are not supported.');e.target.value='';return}if(file.size>500*1024){toast('Choose a file smaller than 500 KB.');e.target.value='';return}try{const text=(await file.text()).replace(/\r\n?/g,'\n').trim();if(!text||text.includes('\0')){toast('This file does not contain readable plain text.');return}importDraft={title:file.name.replace(/\.txt$/i,''),text};go('import-book')}catch{toast('This file could not be read. Try another text file.')}});
const SAMPLE_READING=`The Lantern Keeper

At the edge of the village stood a lantern that nobody remembered lighting. Each evening its small circle of gold appeared on the stone path. Travelers paused beside it, loosened their bags, and looked up at the hill.

Mara had passed the lantern for years. One afternoon she decided to follow the path beyond it. She expected a house, perhaps a workshop. Instead, she found a cave with a wooden bench at its entrance and a kettle resting beside a cold fireplace.

A note on the bench said: “You may sit here. You do not have to explain why.”

She sat. The forest continued around her: a branch settling, a bird testing two notes, water finding its way between stones. Nothing waited for an answer.

After a while she noticed a box of small lanterns beneath the bench. Some were green, some blue, and one had a crooked handle. She chose the crooked one and carried it inside. The cave was not a single room but a collection of spaces, each with its own echo.

In the first room she found a table beneath a narrow opening to the sky. In the second, a pool reflected the ceiling like another world. The third contained nothing at all. Mara liked that room best. There was space to imagine what might belong there.

She placed her lantern on the floor and pictured a chair, a shelf, a window looking onto rain. She laughed at the idea of a window inside a hill. Then she decided that imagination did not have to ask the hill for permission.

When evening arrived, she returned the lantern to its box. Beside the first note she left another: “The empty room is still empty. I think that is part of what makes it lovely.”

Weeks later, she came back with a book. Someone had placed a cushion on the bench. The crooked lantern was still there. She took it into the empty room, opened her book, and began where she had stopped.

Outside, the village lantern glowed. It did not call anyone back. It simply made the path a little easier to find.

The end.`;

